==================================
Analysis of the Hirshfeld atom fit
==================================

The plots are:
. urea.QQ_plot
     a normal QQ plot, each datum labelled by (h k l)
. urea.F_z_vs_stl
     F_z = (F_exp-F_pred)/F_sigma vs sin(theta)/lambda
. urea.F_z_vs_F_exp
     F_z = (F_exp-F_pred)/F_sigma vs F_exp
. urea.Delta_F_vs_stl
     Delta F = (F_exp-F_pred) vs sin(theta)/lambda

If you used an extinction correction, you also get:
. urea.Delta_F_pred_z_vs_stl
     (F_pred(no ext)-F_pred(ext))/F_sigma vs sin(theta)/lambda
. urea.Delta_F_pred_z_vs_F_pred
     (F_pred(no ext)-F_pred(ext))/F_sigma vs F_pred

Each of those is four files: the data itself, then
. <plot>.labels    where the (h k l) labels go, and their leaders
. <plot>.gnuplot   the gnuplot script that draws it
. <plot>.png       the picture, if gnuplot is installed
The QQ plot's data file keeps its older name, urea.QQ_plot_with_hkl.

All of the above are repeated for the reserved (free) data,
headed 'urea.free', if you used that option. Phew.

The pictures are drawn for you if gnuplot is on your PATH; install
it if a .png is missing. To draw one yourself, run the script --
unchanged, it draws to your screen:
   gnuplot -persist 'urea.QQ_plot.gnuplot'

